From 37777eccdbe2c459ece974398d5dd56616bdc843 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Brandon Zylstra <brandon.zylstra@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 19 Aug 2026 23:34:43 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] =?UTF-8?q?=F0=9F=A7=B5=20Make=20PHP=20Fibers=20work?=
 =?UTF-8?q?=20in=20the=20WebAssembly=20build?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Starting a Fiber has aborted the runtime ever since PHP 8.1 shipped. The
build passes ./configure --disable-fiber-asm, because the Boost.Context
assembly PHP normally switches stacks with is x86/ARM machine code and there
is none of that in WebAssembly. That makes PHP fall back to POSIX
getcontext/makecontext/swapcontext, and Emscripten links all three to a stub
that calls abort(), so the first Fiber::suspend() kills the runtime.

Adds ucontext-emscripten.c, which implements the three on top of Emscripten's
own fiber API -- documented as 'similar to, but distinct from, POSIX
ucontext', and asyncify-only, which is the variant this is built for. Two
details keep it small: PHP calls makecontext with zero arguments, so none of
the variadic marshalling is needed, and PHP touches only uc_stack and
uc_link, which leaves the ucontext_t's register-save space (meaningless on
WebAssembly) free to hold the Emscripten fiber. No side table, no allocation,
and so nothing to leak -- which matters, because POSIX has no destroycontext
to hook. The single stack POSIX hands over is split into a C stack low, so an
overflow meets PHP's guard page, and an Asyncify stack high.

Also names the fiber call path in ASYNCIFY_ONLY. A fiber suspends by
unwinding the whole WebAssembly stack, so every frame from Fiber::suspend()
down to swapcontext has to be instrumented; naming a function there is also
what overrides ASYNCIFY_IGNORE_INDIRECT for the function-pointer call that
enters a fiber.

And fixes the abort message on the way past. It was built with single quotes,
so every missing-symbol abort has read literally 'missing function: ${name}'
and named nothing. Emscripten emits a library function by stringifying it,
which drops anything it closed over, so the name is baked into the source
text instead.

Verified in headless Chromium: start, suspend, resume, return values,
suspending from three frames below the entry function, five fibers
interleaving, and throwing into a suspended fiber.
